  "image_name": "f10dc35d805875e088f58cb0e2d676f7.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three methods for making or modifying surface textures on cylindrical samples. At the top, a vibrating setup in vacuum appears to drive small particles repeatedly against a sample surface. The accompanying sketch suggests these impacts or oscillatory motions create a textured, raised surface feature around a contact point.\n\nThe lower two parts show rotating cylindrical samples being contacted by a tool while the sample itself spins. In the middle method, a fixed tip is pressed onto the rotating surface under force. The cylinder rotates in one direction while the tool moves laterally in another, and a thin outer “GNS layer” is indicated on the surface. The right-hand sketch suggests this layer forms through strong near-surface shear generated by the moving contact.\n\nIn the bottom method, a ball-like rolling element held in a lubricated fixture replaces the fixed tip. Here the sample still rotates and translates, but the tool also has its own rotational motion. The schematic implies that lubrication and rolling contact alter the shear conditions compared with the fixed-tip case, again producing a refined surface layer. Overall, the figure emphasizes different mechanical surface treatment strategies and how contact motion controls the resulting modified near-surface structure.",
